# Sprint 3 brief adversarial item #1: "Auth race conditions — sign up + immediately
# try chat before email-verify completes; log out while a stream is in flight;
# refresh token expiry mid-session"

# === Focus: log out while a stream is in flight ===

# Source: Wedding-Ease-Viva-Chat/src/contexts/AuthContext.tsx:205-212

const handleSignOut = async () => {
  track('logout')
  await signOutUser(user?.uid)    // ← Firebase signOut
  setUser(null)
  setProfile(null)
  reset()
  register({ is_authenticated: false })
}

# AuthContext exposes signOut. The chat hook holds an AbortController in
# useChat.ts:203 — abortControllerRef — but only aborts it from the explicit
# user-driven "Stop generation" button (useChat.ts:939-943):
#
#   const stopGeneration = useCallback(() => {
#     const id = activeRequestIdRef.current
#     abortControllerRef.current?.abort()
#     if (id) void cancelChatRequest(id)
#   }, [])
#
# **There is no useEffect / cleanup that aborts the stream when `user` becomes null.**
#
# Sequence:
#   t=0    User sends a long planner message; backend opens a streaming response
#          and starts writing to users/{uidA}/threads/{tid}/messages/{mid}.
#   t=2s   User clicks Logout → handleSignOut runs → setUser(null).
#   t=2.1s onAuthStateChanged fires → AuthContext re-renders.
#   t=3s   Stream keeps yielding tokens because abortControllerRef was never
#          aborted. The frontend setMessages calls keep running.
#   t=5s   Stream completes; backend writes the final assistant message to
#          the previous uid's thread.
#   t=5s   User signs back in as uidB; Index.tsx:818 restores guest chat
#          (uidA's chat is gone from the screen), but the OLD stream's
#          final write is still pending in the browser's microtask queue.
#   t=5.5s setMessages with the AI response runs against uidB's context.
#          If activeThreadId was preserved (same `tid` for some weird reason),
#          the AI text appears under uidB. If not, it appears as a phantom
#          message that vanishes on next thread switch.

# Concrete consequences (from code review only — could not script this race
# without real Firebase auth):
# 1. PHANTOM MESSAGES on the signed-out user's screen for ~3-10s.
# 2. UNNECESSARY BACKEND COST — the Azure OpenAI call continues to completion;
#    user is charged tokens for output they may never see.
# 3. DATA WRITTEN UNDER STALE UID — Firestore writes from the streaming
#    backend land under users/{uidA}/messages/* even though uidA is signed
#    out. Firebase rules will still permit it because the backend uses the
#    Admin SDK with the token from before signOut.
# 4. IF SAME-DEVICE SIGN-IN AS DIFFERENT USER: depending on thread routing
#    luck, the AI reply may cross-bleed into uidB's chat view briefly.

# === Bonus failure: signup-immediate-chat ===
#
# AuthContext.tsx:73-85 — Step 1 reads sessionStorage[SESSION_KEY] and
# short-circuits before onAuthStateChanged even runs. If a fresh signup
# stores SESSION_KEY (the phone-WhatsApp flow does this), then the user's
# email isn't yet verified. quotaCheck middleware allows the request through
# because requireAuth has no emailVerified gate (auth.ts:38-44 only checks
# token signature). Pre-verification chat lands as a fully billable chat
# under uid even though Firebase's emailVerified flag is still false.

# === Fix direction ===
# 1. Add useEffect cleanup in useChat: `useEffect(() => () => abortControllerRef.current?.abort(), [user?.uid])`
# 2. Or: in AuthContext.handleSignOut, dispatch a global event that abortable
#    callers can subscribe to.
# 3. Decide whether to gate chat on emailVerified or not — document the decision.
